WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.670
“Are the blades any good?

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.649
No… our blades are f***ing great”

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:08.280
Michael Dubin is unquestionably a great entrepreneur.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.190
He built Dollar Shave Club from nothing into
a dominant player in the razor market and

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.620
sold the company to Unilever for a billion
dollars.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:23.369
But everyone seems to think he just got lucky
and he tends to perpetuate this idea himself:

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.609
“Luck absolutely factors into it and any
entrepreneur that tells you that they didn’t

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:31.070
get a little lucky or a lot lucky in some
ways is not being truthful probably with you.”

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.800
But I’ve watched Michael Dubin’s career
closely over the past decade, and I don’t

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.250
think luck had anything to do with his success.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.960
He planned and executed every step of Dollar
Shave Club’s growth methodically and always

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.260
played to his strengths.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.640
Sure, he took a non-traditional path to entrepreneurship,
spending years doing standup comedy and working

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.400
at advertising agencies, but plenty of successful
entrepreneurs come from unique backgrounds.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.790
And there are even other successful people
in technology who studied improv, like Dick

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.430
Costolo the former CEO of Twitter.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:05.010
Entrepreneurship is all about playing to your
strengths and nearly any skill can be repurposed

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.870
to have an impact on your business, if used
properly.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.140
Dubin himself admits that his improv background
helped him as an entrepreneur:

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.490
“I think there’s a lot about studying
improv that can help you in business absolutely.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.740
Just the ability to tease out of thin air
the most important ideas and the most resonant

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.010
ideas in any setting is something that you
hone in improv and you don’t have a lot

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.010
of time to do it.”

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.229
Okay, so leveraging whatever is unique about
your background is clearly important, but

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:32.220
that’s still very general advice.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.970
We need to dig deeper to understand exactly
what Dubin did to make Dollar Shave Club so

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:37.970
successful.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.580
Fortunately, the whole story, from idea to
billion dollar sale, can be boiled down to

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.049
3 key strategies, so let’s dive in.

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:47.830
Whenever people discuss Dollar Shave Club,
a lot of attention gets paid to the viral

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.980
launch video that Dubin produced in 2012.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.460
The video got millions of views as soon as
it went live and drove over 12,000 orders,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.880
which crashed their website.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.880
These are amazing results for any product
launch, and naturally, the Dollar Shave Club

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.950
launch video led many entrepreneurs to believe
that they needed a flashy launch video in

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.860
order to announce their business to the world.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.220
But unless you really dissect what’s going
on in the video and how Dubin crafted the

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:15.630
story around Dollar Shave Club, you’ll just
be wasting your time.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:21.550
It’s notoriously hard to make an advertisement
go viral, big brands spend millions of dollars

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.870
producing ads that they hope will break through
the noise, but every year, most ads fall flat

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.280
and only a few are remembered.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.420
But Dubin knew that his ad would get views
because of his first key strategy: He’s

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.110
a master of storytelling.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.900
The idea behind Dollar Shave Club is extremely
simple.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.390
It’s really just low cost razor blades,
shipped to your door regularly, so you don’t

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.569
forget to buy new ones.

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:44.569
That’s it.

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:48.430
But even if you have a really basic business
model, you still need to tell a compelling

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.610
story in order to get your customers' attention.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.980
Let’s breakdown Dollar Shave Club’s launch
video to illustrate what I mean here.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:00.050
Right off the bat, Dubin clearly understands
how short attention spans are on the internet.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:06.020
He introduces himself, asks a question, and
then answers that question in just 10 seconds.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.440
“Hi I’m Mike, founder of DollarShaveClub.com.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.550
What is DollarShaveClub.com?

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:14.970
Well, for a dollar a month, we send high quality
razors right to your door”

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.120
This opening shot is great because it’s
very dynamic, he’s wearing a collared shirt,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.940
but his tie is loose, signaling that he’s
not like other stodgy corporate brands.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.720
There are also a ton of random toys in the
background to add texture to the shot and

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.700
make you think “maybe this guy is a crazy
inventor” - you instantly want to know more.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.670
And he rewards that curiosity immediately
by restating that the blades are just a dollar

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.070
and then asking another question:

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.069
“Are the blades any good?

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:44.290
No… our blades are f***ing great”

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.590
Dubin really leans into his comedy background
and makes sure that he’s injecting humor

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.100
every few seconds.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.140
Even if one joke doesn’t quite land, the
viewer wants to hear the next one, so they

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.140
stick around.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.900
The entire ad is a masterclass in great editing.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.330
Both the camera and the subject are always
moving, which keeps the viewer engaged.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.670
And every scene has an interesting transition
from one area to the next.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.879
You get a clear picture of the warehouse Dubin
is walking through, even while he’s constantly

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:10.970
moving around.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.050
One minute he’s breaking through paper to
deliver the next line, the next minute he’s

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.050
riding on a forklift.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:19.590
Then he’s up on a table, jumping down while
delivering the next point.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.560
The movement is dynamic without being too
frenetic, so it’s extremely hard to click

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.810
away while you’re watching.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.660
And each segment has a little easter egg in
the shot that breaks up the parts where Dubin

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:29.840
is just talking to the camera.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.170
When he talks about how gentle the blades
are, he shows a toddler holding one of the

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.170
razors.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.760
When he talks about how simple blades worked
fine for previous generations, he shows an

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.660
old-timey framed photo.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.910
And when he talks about how DollarShaveClub
ships blades directly to you, he throws the

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.920
package to someone in a bear costume, which
is never explained at all.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.330
Except that maybe hairy bears need shaving?

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.240
When people talk about the success of this
video, they often focus on how incredible

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.030
it was that it was filmed in just one day,
or that it only cost $5,000 dollars to make,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.440
but those facts can be a bit misleading.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.970
Dubin actually spent over a month working
on the script for the video, and he collaborated

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:11.440
with friends of his who had experience writing
great comedy sketches to make sure it would

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.330
land properly.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.960
Storytelling is incredibly important when
you’re launching a consumer brand, especially

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.960
online.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.880
Since your product won’t be on store shelves
immediately, customers won’t be able to

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.970
pick up and inspect your product in the real
world before they decide to buy.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.919
You need to walk them through the narrative
of your business and make sure that message

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:31.270
sticks in their mind.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:36.090
Dubin used humor to grab everyone’s attention
all while delivering key points about why

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.449
Dollar Shave Club was solving a real problem.

00:05:38.449 --> 00:05:40.990
Humor isn’t the only way to tell a powerful
story though.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.419
When we launched Soylent, we told our story
in the form of long-form blog posts that put

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:46.509
a heavy emphasis on data.

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:50.850
We wanted to create a new health-food, so
providing potential customers with detailed

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.509
information about our product and the results
we were personally experiencing really helped

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:56.509
win people over.

00:05:56.509 --> 00:06:01.270
But even though a strong launch can set you
up for success, that alone won’t get you

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.960
to a billion dollar acquisition.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.830
After the hype around your launch dies down,
you need to keep growing, and that’s where

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.600
Dubin’s second key strategy comes in.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.150
He hacked the Facebook algorithm.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.560
Nearly all direct to consumer ecommerce companies
spend money on Facebook, but few actually

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.800
make money from these ads reliably.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.080
This is because Facebook ads are run on an
auction model and as soon as your rivals start

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.200
competing for ad placements, the cost of each
ad skyrockets.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.320
Lots of companies raise venture capital, only
to squander millions of dollars on Facebook

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.860
ads that don’t really move the needle.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.110
They might bring in new customers, but those
customers don’t spend enough money to offset

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:39.509
the cost of the ads.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:43.900
If you’ve ever thought about running online
ads, you’ve probably considered two main

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.700
platforms: Facebook, and Google.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:49.700
Both companies have similar offerings: you
can run a video or image of your product along

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.670
with a simple text description, and then track
how many people see your ad and click through

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:54.670
to your website.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:59.370
Even though Facebook and Google can both be
great sources of growth if used effectively,

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:02.470
the two platforms are more different than
you might think.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.370
Fishing is a useful analogy here.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:08.250
As an entrepreneur, your job is to bring in
as many customers to your business as possible.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:12.680
These customers are like fish, peacefully
swimming along, completely unaware that your

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.259
company even exists.

00:07:14.259 --> 00:07:18.090
You need to catch these fish and you have
two resources at your disposal.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:19.670
The first is a big fishing net.

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:23.449
You can drop this net in the middle of a stream
and just sit there and wait.

00:07:23.449 --> 00:07:27.330
You’ll catch most of the fish that come
through that stream, but if the stream is

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:28.610
empty, you’re out of luck.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.449
Google is that fishing net.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:35.230
Since most Google ads are placed on keyword
searches, customers have to already be searching

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.650
for the product you’re selling.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.480
If you’ve made something completely new,
no one will be searching for it, and Google

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.520
ads won’t really work for your business.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.220
The alternative is a speargun.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.509
It’s more work, since you have to swim out
into the ocean and target individual fish,

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:52.229
but you aim for whatever type of fish you
like, and there are always lots of fish in

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:53.229
the ocean.

00:07:53.229 --> 00:07:54.660
Facebook is the speargun.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:58.620
The customer doesn’t have to be searching
for a specific solution in order to be targeted

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.632
by Facebook ads, you can hand pick a demographic
that you think would be interested in what

00:08:02.632 --> 00:08:07.040
you’ve made, and show them your ad regardless
of what they’re searching for at the time.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.569
Dubin understood this and leveraged Facebook
perfectly.

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:14.789
Before Dollar Shave Club launched, no one
was searching for “razor subscriptions”

00:08:14.789 --> 00:08:16.750
because no one knew they wanted that.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.849
They might have been searching for “best
razors” but that would be highly competitive.

00:08:20.849 --> 00:08:25.210
Instead, Dubin focused on targeting men on
Facebook and educating them on the benefits

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:26.310
of a razor subscription.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.750
But the story doesn’t end there.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.309
Advertisers like DollarShaveClub are successful
on Facebook because they use a strategy called

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:34.699
“sequential targeting.”

00:08:34.699 --> 00:08:38.390
Instead of hitting a potential customer with
an ad asking them to purchase right away,

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:42.360
they start by educating the customer with
a longer, highly produced ad.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.980
You might have seen some of these yourself,
since they tend to stick out in the feed.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.779
There’s one particular ad agency by the
name of Harmon Brothers who’s responsible

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:53.329
for over a billion ad views across the ads
they have produced.

00:08:53.329 --> 00:08:57.801
These ads can cost hundreds of thousands of
dollars to make, but that’s all okay, because

00:08:57.801 --> 00:09:01.959
they are designed to completely dominate the
Facebook algorithm and go viral.

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:06.259
Because viral ads like these are so watchable,
Facebook pushes them into everyone’s feeds

00:09:06.259 --> 00:09:07.870
at a ridiculously low cost.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:12.370
They are genuinely entertaining and people
like watching them, but they actually don’t

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.360
convert to sales all that well.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.679
The secret to these ads is that they are just
meant to expose the maximum amount of people

00:09:18.679 --> 00:09:20.869
to the brand and set up future targeting.

00:09:20.869 --> 00:09:25.970
Facebook knows whether you watched 2 seconds
of a video, or a full minute, and that data

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.449
can be used to decide what ads to show you
next.

00:09:28.449 --> 00:09:32.999
People who watched the full ad get flooded
with follow up ads encouraging them to purchase

00:09:32.999 --> 00:09:37.009
much more directly, and these ads are the
ones that actually make money.

00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:41.910
Dubin’s understanding of this content strategy
was at the heart of his early marketing push

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:46.749
and a big reason why DollarShaveClub was able
to scale so quickly, even after their first

00:09:46.749 --> 00:09:49.930
viral ad had stopped getting organic views.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:54.300
Success on Facebook requires building a highly
specific audience for your product and the

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.130
fastest way to do that is to broadcast your
brand to millions of people and then see who

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.240
paid attention.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.200
Instead of just swimming in the open ocean
with your speargun, you’re effectively chumming

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.499
the water with bait to attract the biggest
fish.

00:10:06.499 --> 00:10:10.489
So if you’re thinking about using Google
or Facebook to promote your new product, it’s

00:10:10.489 --> 00:10:13.709
important to really think about which platform
is right for you.

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:16.059
Are people searching for this product already?

00:10:16.059 --> 00:10:18.389
Or do you need to educate them about it first?

00:10:18.389 --> 00:10:22.119
The answer can tell you a lot about where
you should spend your marketing dollars.

00:10:22.119 --> 00:10:26.300
Dubin is clearly an expert at marketing, and
that makes sense, because he’d worked in

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:31.040
marketing for years before starting DollarShaveClub,
but he also really understands what makes

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.550
a great product.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:36.980
Product design is as much about what you don’t
put in the product as what you do.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:41.389
Dubin saw that all the big razor companies
had been racing to add more and more features

00:10:41.389 --> 00:10:46.160
to their products for years, and this wasn’t
really improving the customer experience.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.209
Many entrepreneurs would assume that the solution
would be to add even more features, like internet

00:10:50.209 --> 00:10:55.420
connectivity and an app to track your shaving
habits, but Dubin realized that simplicity

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.079
was really all that mattered.

00:10:57.079 --> 00:11:01.399
It helped that he only had access to basic
razor inventory, so his hands were somewhat

00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:06.360
tied, but he turned his basic product into
something great with amazing storytelling

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.220
and a simple promise of value to the customer.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:12.959
As an entrepreneur, it can be easy to get
caught up in trends and want to use all the

00:11:12.959 --> 00:11:17.920
latest technology to build your product, but
Dubin focused on using technology only where

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.449
it would actually help the customer, like
the subscription e-commerce platform he built.

00:11:22.449 --> 00:11:26.799
Getting razors delivered on a schedule solved
a problem for customers that the big razor

00:11:26.799 --> 00:11:30.730
companies had never considered, and that set
DollarShaveClub apart.

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:35.079
Ultimately, customers didn’t need fancier
razors, they just needed to replace their

00:11:35.079 --> 00:11:39.699
existing razors more frequently, and DollarShaveClub
made that dead simple to do.

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:44.619
So remember, play to your strengths, tell
a great story, and focus on what’s actually

00:11:44.619 --> 00:11:47.379
important to your customer and your startup
will grow.

00:11:47.379 --> 00:11:51.309
If you want to chat about startups, you can
always DM me on Twitter, and please check

00:11:51.309 --> 00:11:54.720
out this recommended video, the YouTube algorithm
thinks you’ll really like it.

